* {{audio|en|en-au-hit home.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|hit<,,hit> home}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To be especially [[memorable]], [[meaningful]], or [[significant]]; to be fully [[understood]], [[believed]] or [[appreciated]].
#: {{syn|en|strike a chord|strike home}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you think the message really '''hit home''' with him?}}
#: {{ux|en|Baked goodies can really '''hit home''' with a crowd.}}
#* '''2008''', Gordon Watt, ''Fading Home'', AuthorHouse ({{ISBN|9781434362438}}), page 297:
#*: Which led me to publish it, to see if it '''hits home''' at all. Which, to me, is the point of the written word, the novels: Our desire to be reassured and read that, in all this, we're not alone.
# {{lb|en|of an attack, weapon, or projectile}} To [[strike]] its [[target]] with [[damage|damaging]] [[effect]].
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=Battle of Samar - What if TF34 was there?|date=5 May 2021|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=35yLWdYEbZQ|archiveurl=|archivedate=|accessdate=7 August 2022|time=32:32|text=This revised situation doesn't go well for poor old ''Nagato''.  Whilst curls of smoke and scorch marks along the hulls and superstructures of ''Washington'' and ''Alabama'' do tell of ''some'' Japanese shells '''hitting home''', neither of them seem particularly perturbed by this.  Instead, their fire begins to overhaul the Japanese battleship, and, sooner or later, another salvo of 16-inch shells - no one's quite sure whether it comes from ''Alabama'' or ''Washington'' - smashes into the base of the superfiring aft turret.}}

